# Shorts / Reels post workflow

When the user sends a finished clip path (e.g. `e:\Da Vinci\...\BatchA2yt.mp4` or IG twin):

## 0. CapCut AI / lesson-dissect prompts (YT vs IG)

When JD asks for a **CapCut AI prompt** (or similar) to cut Japanese lessons into Shorts/Reels, **plan two platform shapes** — do not treat YT and IG as the same edit:

### Instagram Reels (discovery + follows + saves)
- Lead with a **warning** or a landed **analogy**, not a definition. That’s what got saved and followed.
- Soft thesis / “fastest path” still works only if the first beat is a trap or a picture they can keep.
- Strong IG signal: high saves, non-follower reach, profile follows.

### YouTube Shorts (feed is pickier)
- Lean **concrete mistake → quiz → fix** (“I hear this mistake…”, opposite of X?, wrong vs right).
- Abstract fluency philosophy often **flops** on Shorts unless the first second is a sharp problem.
- Shorts-native: conjugation puzzle, te-form fail, specific wrong answer.
- If reusing a storytelling lesson for YT, **re-prompt CapCut** to open on a specific fail before the thesis.

### What to hunt in a lesson (3 types)

When dissecting a lesson into clips, **look for these three**. Don’t start by cutting “what this word means.” Sep 2026 IG: warnings got saved and followed; meaning clips watched and moved on.

1. **Warning** — hunt this first. He flags a trap: don’t, doesn’t mean, be careful, this is where you mess up, can’t say it that way. The save is the stop sign, not the definition. (`どこにも` doesn’t mean anything by itself. Don’t translate `いつ` as English “when.”)
2. **Analogy** — yes, pick these out. The tell is a **map onto something else**, not a definition. Listen for “it’s like”, “think of it as”, “same as when you…”, a picture, a habit, an English sentence he holds up beside the Japanese. If he only explains what the word means, that is a meaning, not an analogy. When you find one, name the line and the thing he’s comparing it to so he can confirm the catch.
3. **Wrong then right** — he says the bad line out loud, then the good one (or a quiz beat, then the fix). Different from a warning: a warning is the stop; this is the pair. YouTube wants this in the first second. Instagram still opens on the warning if both are in the same stretch.

**Meanings** (what a word means, a grammar label, a pattern explained) are leftover. Clip them only if the lesson has no warning, analogy, or wrong-then-right — and say so. Don’t lead a batch with them.

When listing cuts, tag each one `warning` / `analogy` / `wrong-then-right` and put warnings first.

### When writing CapCut prompts
- Prefer **separate YT vs IG prompt variants** (or one prompt with explicit “YT cut” / “IG cut” instructions).
- Cold open: warning or the wrong line — not a meaning.
- Call out cold open, on-screen text style, and pacing per platform using the above.
- Keep pedagogy: mistake→fix in captions is intentional — don’t “correct” it away.

### Anime-clip teaches (different format)
When the source is an **anime line** (or similar clip) and JD is teaching that line: use the **anime dual-pane** layout in `anime-clip-teach-format.mdc` — face top, anime bottom, white JP+romaji card, yellow EN card. He **explains** grammar/nuance; do not copy “repeat + translate only” creators.

## 1. Review the clip
- Prefer `ffprobe` + a few sample frames if tools are available.
- Suggest improvements **only** if something actually needs changing.
- Specs preference: **1080×1920 H.264 AAC MP4**.
- Do **not** “fix” intentional student-mistakes-in-captions (mistake→fix pedagogy).
- If they send YT-only (or IG-only), lightly ask/remind about the twin export.
- Clip numbering / filenames: **leave as-is**. The **title alone** is enough to identify which clip — no extra “Batch A2” labels needed in pings unless the user asks.

## 2. Paste-ready copy (separate labeled blocks)
Offer wording is **Book a trial lesson** (not “free consultation”; not Homework Hub unless user says otherwise).
CTA / Link sticker URL (always this — contact form, **not** availability):

`https://japaneselanguagementor.com/#contact`

| Platform | Block | Notes |
|----------|--------|--------|
| YouTube | **Title** | Clear, hooky (identifies the clip) |
| YouTube | **Description** | Light hashtags + Book a trial lesson URL. On-screen cue: Book a trial lesson + japaneselanguagementor.com |
| YouTube | **Pin comment** | Always include — `Book a trial lesson →` + URL; note Shorts description links aren’t reliably clickable |
| Instagram | **Caption** | CTA = link in bio; hashtags at end. End line: `Book a trial lesson → link in bio` |
| Instagram | **Story caption** | Always include a **short** Story text. Default: `Book a trial lesson ↑` (Link sticker sits above the caption) |
| Instagram | **Link sticker URL** | Always include the `#contact` URL above for the Story Link sticker |

When he asks for the post info, send it to the **video notifications** Discord channel (`1555467510281805844`). One message per clip. Each message has only:

- YouTube **title**
- YouTube **description**
- Instagram **caption**

Do not dump those blocks in chat. One line that they were sent is enough.

Do **not** include the YT pin comment, IG Story caption, or Link sticker URL. Those go in the later Discord/Hub ping when the reminder is armed. Still keep them ready internally (defaults below).

## 3. Arm reminder → Cloudflare (not PC sleeper)

**Manual ping prompt required.** Do **not** auto-arm after clip review.
Only arm when JD explicitly asks (e.g. “arm ping”, “ping at 10:30”, drops a YT link + “remind me”, or “set up vid pings for today”).
Clip review alone never schedules Discord.

**Easiest remind method for JD:** paste the **YouTube Shorts/video URL** (scheduled or live) and say to arm/ping.
- Prefer reading the **scheduled publish time** from the link when tools can (e.g. `yt-dlp --print` / page meta).
- If schedule can’t be read, ask once for fire time (JP or LA), then arm.
- Title in the ping = the video’s title (or the paste-ready YT title from review).

- Default slot **only when they ask to arm without a time:** **Wed / Fri at 9:00 AM JST** (JD's standing schedule as of Oct 2026 — 2 posts/week). Two clips/week → Wed + Fri 9:00am JST; one → Wed 9:00am JST. Or the exact time they gave (e.g. `8/6 10:30am jp`).
  - 9:00am JST ≈ **5:00pm PDT (prev. day)** / 4:00pm PST — anchor is **Japan time now**, not Pacific.
  - Legacy notes: older defaults were 6:30pm LA, then Insights-primetime Wed/Sat/Sun 3:00pm — do **not** use those unless JD asks.
- Convert to **UTC ISO** carefully (JST = UTC+9; LA PDT = UTC−7 / PST = UTC−8).
- If multiple clips same day: **one** ping listing all titles — do not Discord-spam.
- After arming, confirm fire time in user language (JP + LA) + job id.
- Optional phone alarm backup still fine; **PC does not need to stay awake**.
- Never echo webhook secrets; never commit `.dev.vars`.

### 3a. How to arm (agent)
Prefer production API via script (after Worker is deployed):

```bash
node scripts/arm-social-reminder.mjs --fire 2026-08-06T01:30:00Z --titles "Want Disneyland? hoshii vs -tai" --story "Book a trial lesson ↑"
```

Or `POST https://japaneselanguagementor.com/api/social-reminders` with JSON:

```json
{
  "teacherUsername": "jlm",
  "fireAtUtc": "2026-08-06T01:30:00.000Z",
  "clipTitles": "...",
  "ytPinComment": "Book a trial lesson → https://japaneselanguagementor.com/#contact",
  "igStoryCaption": "Book a trial lesson ↑",
  "linkSticker": "https://japaneselanguagementor.com/#contact"
}
```

List / cancel:

```bash
node scripts/arm-social-reminder.mjs --list
node scripts/arm-social-reminder.mjs --cancel sr-...
```

Worker cron `* * * * *` loads `sr-pending:*` from `HOMEWORK_KV` and fires due jobs.

### 3b. Discord + Teacher Hub payload (fired by Worker)
**One Discord message** with **four separate copyable code blocks** (title, pin, Story caption, link sticker):

````
```
{video title}
```
```
{yt pin comment}
```
```
{ig story caption}
```
```
